  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Labs, clinics and firms pay for accurate results and clear reporting. They judge you by method, records and reproducibility.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is a one-page method note that someone else could repeat.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and genetics and genomics: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools now predict structures and analyse sequences. Routine analysis shrinks first.
  </p>
  <p>
    Experimental design, quality checks and interpretation hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI tools to explore; later, own design and validation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general genetics and genomics path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Biotechnology route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Broader</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Less genetics focus</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Data science route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Wider employers</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs coding</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Clinical lab technology</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">More direct hiring</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Narrower</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn genetics and genomics cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Genetics and genomics is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free public sequence databases and tutorials.</li>
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM genetics and bioinformatics lessons.</li>
    <li>Free coding lessons.</li>
    <li>A lab visit or a scientist's time.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In four weeks, run one small public-data analysis, write the method note and ask a scientist to review it.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="streams">Three streams inside genetics</h2>

  <p>
    Genetics is not one job. Know which stream fits your temperament.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Stream</th>
          <th>Daily work</th>
          <th>Best for people who</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Stream"><strong>Wet lab</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Daily work">Extract, amplify and analyse samples</td>
          <td data-label="Best for people who">Like precise hands-on work</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Stream"><strong>Clinical and diagnostics</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Daily work">Run tests, interpret reports with clinicians</td>
          <td data-label="Best for people who">Like patient-linked work and accuracy</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Stream"><strong>Computational</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Daily work">Analyse sequence data with code</td>
          <td data-label="Best for people who">Like data, coding and patterns</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Stream"><strong>Counselling-linked</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Daily work">Explain results to families</td>
          <td data-label="Best for people who">Like people work (separate training applies)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Course names and requirements vary by institute. Check which streams a course supports before you join.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ethics">Ethics and data handling you should learn early</h2>

  <p>
    Genetic data is personal. Good habits protect people and your reputation.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Issue</th>
          <th>Good practice</th>
          <th>Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Issue"><strong>Consent</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good practice">Use data only with proper permission</td>
          <td data-label="Why">People own their information</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Issue"><strong>Privacy</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good practice">Remove identifiers and store data securely</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Prevents harm</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Issue"><strong>Accuracy</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good practice">Document methods and check results</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Errors can affect health decisions</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Issue"><strong>Honesty</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good practice">Report limits and uncertainty</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Builds trust</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Issue"><strong>Rules</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Good practice">Learn the institute and legal rules for your work</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Rules differ and change</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Employers in this field look for people who treat these points as normal practice.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pipeline">From a sample to a result: where each skill sits</h2>

  <p>
    Seeing the whole path helps you decide which part you want to own.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Step</th>
          <th>What happens</th>
          <th>Skill that matters most</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Step"><strong>Collection</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What happens">A sample is taken with consent and labelled</td>
          <td data-label="Skill that matters most">Care and ethics</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Step"><strong>Preparation</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What happens">DNA or RNA is extracted and checked</td>
          <td data-label="Skill that matters most">Lab precision</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Step"><strong>Measurement</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What happens">A test or sequencing run produces raw data</td>
          <td data-label="Skill that matters most">Instrument handling</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Step"><strong>Analysis</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What happens">Software aligns and compares the data</td>
          <td data-label="Skill that matters most">Coding and statistics</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Step"><strong>Interpretation</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What happens">A scientist or clinician decides what it means</td>
          <td data-label="Skill that matters most">Domain knowledge and caution</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Step"><strong>Reporting</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What happens">Results are written in plain language</td>
          <td data-label="Skill that matters most">Clear writing</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Ask people in the field which step they would hand to a machine first and which they would never hand over.
  </p>
  <p>
    A student project that covers even two of these steps, such as analysis and reporting on public data, is a strong starting proof.
  </p>

  <h2 id="money">Money: course fees and the 10 percent test</h2>

  <p>
    As a rule of thumb and not a research finding, keep a degree or course fee near a tenth of the family's total education budget, and treat spending above roughly a fifth as a serious caution that needs course-specific evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: on a Rs 8 lakh education budget, a tenth is Rs 80,000 for the main course, and a fifth is Rs 1.6 lakh as a serious caution. The rest can fund a laptop, practice time and a reserve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test the field with free courses and one finished project before paying for a long programme. Do not borrow heavily for a course whose graduates cannot show placement in a specific role.
  </p>

